## Reviewed recommendation, not an accepted policy

The 4 October export does not support treating [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) as
one proven cause with one fix. It contains two different date contracts going wrong.
This analysis explains the split and its closure outcomes below; the support desk
must still approve any operational policy replacement. The old incident's
“one issue, one fix queued” claim is not a safe description of this export.

<Callout>
<a href="/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject">Decision 0001</a> and the
<a href="/kb/triage/dedupe">subject-only triage rule</a> remain accepted historical records,
unchanged here. Their subject-only merge instruction is outdated for this case:
matching symptoms or subject lines do not establish matching causes.
This page proposes a replacement approach; it does not silently accept one.
</Callout>

## What the export actually counts

<SplitSummary />

Counts run through the supplied `uniqueById`, `groupByCause` and tenant lookup
in `locales.ts`. The classifier uses tenant locale, booking form and report
channel, not the reporter's browser language. These are model assignments:
not every assigned report independently proves its proposed cause.

## Two defects, two engineering issues

- **[Shared parser: issue #1](https://github.com/andrzej1990m/mega-s01e03/issues/1).**
  At the time of the export, the shared parser read month first for day-first tenants.
  For example,
  R-41200 (`harbour.dental`, app) says a 7 April reminder went out on 4 July.
  The pre-fix reproduction was `07/04/2026`: the tenant meant 7 April, but month-first
  parsing yielded 4 July. Retain this as a regression case for shared-parser paths.
- **[Booking-v3 hint: issue #2](https://github.com/andrzej1990m/mega-s01e03/issues/2).**
  Booking-v3 displayed `MM/DD/YYYY`, prompting month-first input, while the
  form interpreted it in the tenant's day-first order. R-41230 (`copper.studio`,
  form) reports 1 June instead of 6 January. Entering `01/06/2026` following that
  hint reproduced the mismatch. The form was subsequently retired, not corrected;
  the parser fix alone did not resolve this separate hint defect.

## Closure outcomes

The course update records that **#4471 closed on 2026-10-05**, with separate outcomes:

- Parser — **FIXED: fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales**.
- Hint — **`WON'T FIX: booking-v3 form retired`**.

Issue #1 is closed completed; issue #2 is closed not_planned. Both published issues
carry resolution comments. These outcomes do not mean both defects were fixed,
nor do they independently prove every report's assigned cause. They also do not
accept a replacement desk policy.

## Uncertainty stays visible

The desk-noted reports above describe one-day, calendar-boundary or delivery-timing
symptoms, not demonstrated month/day transpositions. Keep them within their current
parser assignment pending investigation. They do **not** prove a timezone/DST
defect, nor a third root cause; do not discard them or count them again.
Other assignments also need case-level confirmation. Locale and channel explain
the model's grouping, not a verified reproduction for every report.

## Proposed desk workflow

1. Keep one report per ID. Read the body, intended date, resulting date and channel;
   look up the tenant's locale and booking form. Do not infer the contract from
   browser locale or `meta.intakeTag`.
2. Compare reproduction paths and causes before merging. Route shared-parser
   historical evidence to issue #1 and booking-v3 hint evidence to issue #2,
   retaining their distinct closed outcomes, original report IDs and a
   cross-reference to #4471. For new reports, establish the current path before
   associating them with either closed issue. A matching subject is
   a search aid, not a merge decision; different subjects can still share a cause.
3. If evidence is insufficient, mark the assignment provisional and ask for the
   input, expected/output dates and path. Investigate the desk-noted cases without
   inventing a third bucket or inflating counts.
4. Ask the support desk to approve a new decision replacing the subject-only rule
   before adopting this as policy. Preserve accepted decision 0001 as history.
   Record each defect's outcome independently; ticket closure does not mean every
   defect was fixed.

Source: `reports-4471.json`, tenant directory, `desk-notes.json`, `causes.ts` and
`locales.ts` in the supplied support KB export (2026-10-04); the course closure
update and published issue resolution comments supply the later outcomes.
The computed counts remain an export snapshot, not a live unresolved queue.

